# How this wiki is kept

This file is for whoever edits the wiki. To use the wiki, start at [index.md](index.md).

An agent writes this wiki and keeps it current in the same change as the code it describes (the "LLM Wiki" pattern, [Karpathy](https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f)), and a lint checks it against the code.

| Layer | What it is | Who changes it |
|---|---|---|
| **Sources** | {{the code, tests and captured outputs, by folder}} | developers; the wiki never edits them |
| **Wiki** | the root `README.md` (the front page) and the pages in `{{wiki_dir}}/` | the agent, in the same change as the code |
| **Schema** | this file | the maintainers and the agent, together |

## Pages

| Readers see | Folder | `kind:` | What goes there |
|---|---|---|---|
| Start here | the root `README.md` | | what it is; how to set it up, run, test and debug it |
| How it works | `concepts/` | explanation | `overview.md`: the parts, and the code to know first. `how-<flow>-works.md`: one core flow's steps, methods, calls and rules |
| Guides | `howto/` | how-to | how do I …; `run-and-debug.md` has where to break and the symptom table |
| Reference | `reference/`{{, and the folders for this project's subsystems}} | reference | what exactly does X do; `map.md` has one row per module with the function to open first, and where the code is not what it seems |
| Tutorials | `start/` | tutorial | walk me through it once |

```text
---
title: <name>
kind: tutorial | how-to | reference | explanation
sources: [<path>#<Symbol>, …]          # the code this page describes
updated: YYYY-MM-DD
---
# <name>
One or two sentences: what this page answers.
```

## Rules

1. **Update in the same change.** A change that alters behaviour, setup, commands or layout updates every page whose `sources:` name a changed file or symbol (`wiki.py affected` lists them), and the README when setup or commands change. A new module gets a row on `reference/map.md`, a new part of the system goes on `concepts/overview.md`, and a new page goes in `index.md`.
2. **Log every change.** Append one entry at the end of `log.md`: `## [YYYY-MM-DD] <build|change|lint|decision> | <title>`, then one to three lines naming, by path, the pages updated and the pages checked and still true (`Updated: … Checked: …`). Naming a page records the check, so the lint stops reporting it as out of date.
3. **Point to symbols, not line numbers**, for example `{{an example path#symbol from this repository}}`.
4. **Every claim is traceable** to a source symbol or a captured output. Captured examples are copied from a real run or a file ({{where captured evidence lives}}) and say where they came from; any other example is labelled illustrative.
5. **One home per fact.** A table, list or procedure is written in full on one page; another page gives the part its task needs, and a link.
6. **Write for a newcomer.** Headings say the point in the reader's words. A section opens with what to do or expect, with one example; the mechanism follows. Steps in order, a choice or a comparison are shown as a flowchart or a table, not told in a paragraph.
7. **Diagrams.** A fenced `mermaid` block whose first line is `%% id: <name>`, every label in double quotes without semicolons or angle brackets. The page shows `diagrams/<name>.svg` and keeps the source in a collapsed `details` block under it. A box that names code ends with a `file: function` line. On a call map (second line `%% calls`) a solid arrow is a direct call and a dotted one an indirect call. Keep a diagram within a Markdown column, about 860 px: chains top to bottom, long labels broken with `<br/>`. Run `wiki_browser.py render` after every edit.
8. **Formulas.** A formula the reader must follow goes in a fenced `math` block (TeX), with one sentence before it on what it computes and where, and a table of its symbols with the example's values after it. Not `$$`.
9. **Commands that must keep working.** Every shell block is marked: `<!-- check:run -->` runs it in a temporary clean checkout (`os=…`, `exit=<code>`, `background ready=…` where needed); `<!-- check:output -->` above the output shown after a command makes the real output match it; `<!-- check:skip reason="…" -->` says why a block cannot run.
10. **Sources the code follows.** A standard, handbook, paper or specification is listed once on `reference/sources.md`, and every mention links its row there or the repository's own chapter on it.
11. **A warning that stays** is kept with its reason on the page it is about: `<!-- lint:accept "part of the warning's message" reason="why it stays" -->`.
12. **Keep good answers.** An answer about this repository that took real exploration and will be asked again is written on the page it belongs on, and listed in `questions.md` with a link there.
13. **The wiki describes the code; it never changes it.** A code change that would help readers is proposed separately.

The lint checks links, the symbols that `sources:`, tables and diagrams name, the calls a call map draws, the sources pages cite, how much of the code the pages and the map lead to, the diagrams' freshness, the log, and with `--run` the commands. A periodic review adds what a script cannot check: claims the code no longer supports, a fact kept on two pages, contradictions between pages{{, and with the design documents: …}}.

## Commands

Run from the repository root. The tools in `{{tools_dir}}/` need only Python 3.9 or later; rendering diagrams and checking the viewer also need Chrome, Edge or Python Playwright.

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Which pages a change touches | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki.py affected` |
| Lint | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki.py lint --strict` |
| Lint and run the documented commands as a newcomer would | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki.py lint --run --strict --clean-env` |
| Render the diagrams | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki_browser.py render` |
| A call map read from Python code | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki.py callgraph <path>#<function>` (`--table` for the complete `Caller | Calls` list) |
| Write captured outputs into blocks marked `check:output fill` | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki.py fill --capture <folder from lint --run --capture>` |
| Questions asked in sessions here, not answered yet | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki.py questions` |
| Build the browsable viewer | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki.py viewer` |
| Check the viewer like a reader | `python {{tools_dir}}/wiki_browser.py check-viewer {{wiki_dir}}/viewer.html` |
